      "notes": [
        "**Georgian counts in twenties, not tens.** ოცდაათი (30) literally means \"twenty and ten\". ორმოცი (40) = \"two times twenty\". French does the same (*quatre-vingts* = 80), and so does Danish — and English once had \"four score and seven\". It looks strange, but once you see the pattern, ten words are all you need for the first hundred numbers.",
        "**ოცდახუთი = 25** — ოცი (20) + და (and) + ხუთი (5). Notice that the parts merge into a single word with no spaces. Georgian numbers are always written as one word: ორმოცდაათი (50), სამოცდაცხრამეტი (79).",
        "**კილო needs no ending at all.** *ორი კილო ატამი* — two kilos of peaches, everything in dictionary form. No \"of\", and unlike Russian or German, no ending changes.",
        "**ძვირია / იაფია — expensive / cheap.** To bargain politely, don't name the price you want; say *ცოტა ძვირია* (it's a bit expensive) and wait. ⚠️ At Georgian food markets, hard bargaining is **not** usual — the price per kilo is generally fixed. What can change is the quantity and the extras.",
        "**საჩუქრად = \"as a gift\".** The **-ად** ending you know from unit 3 (ქართულ**ად**), now meaning \"as / into\". Getting a free extra at a Georgian market is an everyday thing, not a special favor — turning it down actually comes across as stiff.",
        "**ხურდა = change (money back).** An important sentence if you pay with a big note: *ხურდა გაქვთ?* (do you have change?). In Tbilisi, paying for a bus ticket with a 50-lari note will always be refused.",
        "**რა თქმა უნდა** (ra tkma unda) — \"of course\". Literally \"what needs to be said\". One of the most common phrases in everyday conversation; learn it as a whole."
      ],
