        {
          "type": "kotak",
          "title": "Six sentences for a whole market visit",
          "items": [
            "**ეს რა ღირს?** — How much is this?",
            "**ერთი კილო, თუ შეიძლება.** — One kilo, please.",
            "**ნახევარი კილო.** — Half a kilo.",
            "**ცოტა ძვირია.** — It's a bit expensive. (the most polite way to bargain)",
            "**ხურდა გაქვთ?** — Do you have change?",
            "**სულ რამდენი გამოვიდა?** — How much altogether?"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "One word that isn't on any list but you'll hear all the time: **გენაცვალე** (genatsvale). Market vendors, taxi drivers, and grandmothers use it with strangers, and it can't really be translated — roughly \"dear\", \"sweetheart\", or \"may I take your place (in suffering)\". If someone calls you genatsvale, you're being treated warmly."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "⚠️ About prices: labels in Georgian shops almost always show the price **per kilo**, not per pack — even for cheese and meat. A 30 ₾ label on a small piece of cheese most likely means thirty lari a kilo, and the piece itself costs much less. The sentence *ეს ცალი რა ღირს?* (how much is this piece?) clears up the confusion."
        }
      ]
